-- title:
--   The coordinate jmath̃ has order -1 at the place at infinity
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field. Inside the field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(k)$ of Laurent series over $k$ consider the level-one modular function field `modularFunctionFieldC k 1`, the intermediate field of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(k)/k$ generated by the two series `jqModC k` and `jqNModC k 1`; here `jqModC k` is $q^{-1}$ times the image in $k[[q]]$ of the integral power series `jNum` $=$ `eisenstein4`$^3\cdot$`dedekindEtaUnitInv`. On the rational function field $k(X)$ take `RationalFunctionField.placeInfty k`, the place given by the valuation subring of the infinite valuation `RatFunc.inftyValuation k` (a valuation subring containing $k$, different from everything, whose ring is a principal ideal ring), and transport it by `charLGeomPlaceEquiv k`, the bijection on places induced by the $k$-algebra isomorphism `ratFuncEquivCharLOneC k : RatFunc k ≃ₐ[k] modularFunctionFieldC k 1` that sends $X$ to the transcendental element `jqModC k`. The assertion is that for the resulting place $v$ of `modularFunctionFieldC k 1` one has $v.\mathrm{ord}(\tilde\jmath)=-1$, where $\tilde\jmath$ denotes `jqModC k` viewed as an element of that field and $v.\mathrm{ord}(f)=-\log$ of the value of $f$ under the adic valuation attached to the height-one prime of the valuation subring of $v$.
--
--   This is the statement that the level-one moduli coordinate $\tilde\jmath$ has a simple pole at the cusp, in the form of an exact order computation at the place at infinity of the $\tilde\jmath$-line over an arbitrary field. It is used to read off pole orders of polynomials in $\tilde\jmath$ at the cusp, for instance in the analysis of the multiplicative covering and of Hasse exponents on the components of $X_0(p)$ in characteristic $p$.
-- source:
